W("Reference to context: 'Horace Danby was good and respectable - but not completely honest.' (i) What was dishonest about him? (ii) Why 'respectable'? (iii) What does the word 'but' do?", 3, "(i) He robbed a safe every year. (1) (ii) Everyone thought him a good citizen: he ran a successful lock-making business, had two helpers and lived quietly with his housekeeper. (1) (iii) It introduces the contrast between public image and secret habit. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'All you had to do to keep dogs quiet was to call them by their right names, and show them love.' (i) Whose thought is this? (ii) What does it tell us about him? (iii) How does the lady use the dog?", 3, "(i) Horace's. (1) (ii) He is gentle and kind to animals; he is not a violent thief. (1) (iii) She is rubbed against by Sherry, which makes Horace believe she belongs to the house. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'How foolish people are when they own valuable things.' (i) What made him think so? (ii) What irony is there? (iii) What 'foolishness' will he show later?", 3, "(i) A magazine article had given a plan of all the rooms with a picture of the room and had even mentioned that the painting hid a safe. (1) (ii) Horace considers others foolish but is himself fooled by the lady. (1) (iii) He trusts a stranger, takes off his gloves, and leaves fingerprints. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'I have always liked the wrong kind of people.' (i) Who says it? (ii) Why? (iii) Is she sincere?", 3, "(i) The young woman in red. (1) (ii) Pretending to sympathise with Horace after hearing his plea about prison, she hints that she will let him go. (1) (iii) No; it is part of her act, to gain his confidence while she makes him do her job. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'Society must be protected from men like you.' (i) Who says it? (ii) How does Horace reply? (iii) What does the answer tell us about him?", 3, "(i) The lady. (1) (ii) 'I'm not a man who threatens society. I steal only from those who have a lot of money. I steal for a very good reason.' (1) (iii) He has a code and a justification, and wants to be seen as a gentleman burglar. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'He gets very angry when anyone talks about honour among thieves.' (i) Why? (ii) What does 'honour among thieves' mean? (iii) How is the irony shown?", 3, "(i) Because the 'young lady', herself a thief, tricked him. (1) (ii) The belief that thieves will be loyal and honest with one another. (1) (iii) She exploited his trust and left him to pay for her crime. (1)", "M"),
W("How did Horace plan his yearly robberies? Write four points from the text.", 3, "He planned carefully, studied the house (rooms, electric wiring, paths, garden) for two weeks, chose a day when the servants were away, used gloves to avoid fingerprints, and sold the jewels one by one. (3)", "M"),
W("Describe how the lady deceives Horace. Mention three things she does.", 3, "She says she heard him sneezing from the top of the house; she is greeted by the dog and calls him 'Down, Sherry'; she says that she came back for her jewels and had forgotten the numbers; she seems calm and amused, which persuades Horace she is the lady of the house. (3)", "M"),
W("Why did Horace not suspect that the lady was an impostor? Give two reasons.", 3, "The dog Sherry, whom he had befriended, was friendly to her. She spoke with confidence and authority, and claimed to be one of the family. He was also eager to be let off, so he accepted a convenient story without testing it. (3)", "M"),
W("Vocabulary in context: Explain 'hay fever' from the glossary and say how it worked against Horace.", 2, "Hay fever is a disorder of the nose and throat caused by allergy to pollen or dust. The flowers in the room made him sneeze, and so he was distracted and was heard by the lady. (2)", "M"),
W("Vocabulary in context: 'The voice was suddenly sharp.' What does 'sharp' mean here, and what had changed?", 2, "'Sharp' means harsh and severe. The lady's voice, which had been kind and amused, changed when Horace asked her to let him go, as she told him that society must be protected. (2)", "M"),
W("Vocabulary in context: 'Horace was a meticulous planner.' Explain 'meticulous' and give evidence from the text.", 2, "'Meticulous' means extremely careful about details. He studied the house for two weeks, cut the wire of the burglar alarm and arranged his tools carefully. (2)", "M"),
W("Grammar in context: Change into indirect speech: The lady said, 'I would telephone the police.'", 2, "The lady said that she would telephone the police. (2)", "M"),
W("Grammar in context: Rewrite using the passive voice: 'A policeman arrested him.'", 1, "He was arrested by a policeman. (1)", "M"),
W("Grammar in context: Identify the sentence type and fill in the verb: 'If I let you go, you (rob) someone else.'", 2, "A first conditional (real condition). 'If I let you go, you will rob someone else.' (1+1)", "M"),
W("Grammar in context: Join with 'although': He was a respectable man. He robbed a safe every year.", 2, "Although he was a respectable man, he robbed a safe every year. (2)", "M"),
W("Character: Write three qualities of the lady and one detail to prove each.", 3, "Clever: she thinks quickly and plays the part of the owner's wife. Confident: she walks to the fireplace and straightens the ornaments. Deceitful: she lies about the jewels and the party to make Horace do her work. (1+1+1)", "M"),
W("Character: Is Horace a typical thief? Explain in 40-50 words.", 3, "No. He loves rare books, lives respectably, steals only once a year from the rich and hates the thought of prison. He is kind to a dog and does not wish to harm anyone. He is a gentle, planned burglar rather than a criminal by nature. (3)", "M"),
W("Error analysis: A student writes, 'Horace was arrested because the owner's wife recognised him at the party.' Find the mistake.", 3, "The owner's wife was not at the party and did not recognise him. The police arrested him because his fingerprints were all over the room (he had opened the safe without gloves), and the real owner's wife, gray-haired and sixty, denied sending anyone. (3)", "M"),
