 ("p", "**Extract 1:** 'Fifteen years ago, Horace had served his first and only sentence in a prison library. He loved rare, expensive books. So he robbed a safe every year.' This explains his strange habit. The humour lies in the cause-and-effect: prison gave him a love of books and the crime that pays for them."),
 ("p", "**Extract 2:** 'You can cure it with a special treatment, you know, if you find out just what plant gives you the disease.' The lady's kindly advice is a clever trick: it makes Horace feel at ease and respect her as a caring, authoritative person while he is caught red-handed."),
 ("p", "**Extract 3:** 'He gets very angry when anyone talks about honour among thieves.' The last line answers the question at the start of the story: the lady 'tricked' him. It is also a joke, because Horace calls himself a thief who expected loyalty."),
 ("p", "**Extract 4:** 'Just leave it to me and you'll have them within an hour. But I'll have to break your safe.' Horace, the expert, is delighted to be asked, since opening safes is his skill. The careless line 'Don't worry about that. My husband won't be here for a month' shows the lady's quick answers; she has an explanation for every question, which is the mark of a planned deception rather than a chance meeting."),
